Extract an archive, or the selected entries. Unsafe entries (paths climbing out with `..`,
links leading out) are skipped and the rest extracted; the command then fails with `Unsafe`
and the report in `details`. Without `dest`, files go into the current directory.

Usage: beezip extract [OPTIONS] <ARCHIVE> [ENTRIES]...

Arguments:
  <ARCHIVE>
          The archive

  [ENTRIES]...
          Entries to extract (a folder takes everything under it). All when none are given

Options:
  -o, --dest <DEST>
          Where to extract [default: the current directory]

      --glob <PATTERN>
          Extract only entries matching these patterns. Without `/` a pattern matches a name at any
          depth (`*.txt`); with `/` the whole path (`docs/**/*.md`)

      --exclude <PATTERN>
          Leave out entries matching these patterns

      --strip-components <N>
          Drop this many leading path components, as with `tar --strip-components`

      --smart[=<SMART>]
          If the selection has more than one top-level item, extract into a folder named after the
          archive (`a.tar.zst` → `a/`), as Explorer's "Extract here" does
          
          [possible values: true, false]

      --overwrite <OVERWRITE>
          What to do with files that exist [default: from config, `ask`]

          Possible values:
          - ask:    Ask (in a terminal or the app); fail with `Conflict` when nobody can be asked
          - always
          - never
          - rename: Write `name (1).ext` instead
          - newer:  Replace only if the entry is newer

      --links[=<LINKS>]
          Create symlinks that stay inside the destination [default: from config, off]. Hard links
          are always extracted as copies
          
          [possible values: true, false]

  -p, --password <PASSWORD>
          The password for encrypted entries

      --name-encoding <ENCODING>
          How names stored as bytes are read: zip names without the UTF-8 flag, tar names outside
          pax records [default: from config, `auto`]
          
          [possible values: auto, utf-8, cp437, cp850, cp852, cp866, cp874, cp932, cp936, cp949,
          cp950, cp1250, cp1251, cp1252, cp1253, cp1254, cp1255, cp1256, cp1257, cp1258, koi8-r,
          koi8-u, iso-8859-1, iso-8859-2, iso-8859-5, iso-8859-7, euc-jp, gb18030]

      --dry-run[=<DRY_RUN>]
          Show what would be extracted, and where, without writing anything
          
          [possible values: true, false]

  -h, --help
          Print help (see a summary with '-h')

Global options:
      --output <FORMAT>
          How to print results; ndjson streams every job event

          Possible values:
          - human:  Text for people
          - json:   One JSON document on stdout: the output, or `{"error": {...}}`
          - ndjson: Every job event as a JSON line on stdout, ending with `Finished`, `Failed` or
            `Cancelled`
          
          [default: human]

  -q, --quiet
          No progress bars or info messages; warnings still print
